---
name: complete-and-consistent
description: Deliver complete, consistent changes even if it takes longer. Partial or inconsistent work is a failure, not progress.
---

## Core Principle

**Complete and consistent changes only. Partial or inconsistent work is failure, not progress.** Multi-file changes: find every occurrence first (tests, parallel dirs, variant names, docs), change all of them identically, verify the old pattern is gone.

Be concise in all interactions and commit messages. Sacrifice grammar for brevity.
End each plan with a list of unresolved questions (if any). Keep questions brief.

## User Habits

Vim user — reflexively hits Esc as a "reset to normal mode" gesture with no intent to cancel. Esc is unbound from `chat:cancel` in `~/.claude/keybindings.json` (Chat context). Ctrl-C is the interrupt key. Editor mode stays on `normal` (not vim mode), because vim mode makes slash commands trigger search.

**Commit when green.** Finish a logical unit, run the focused tests (the files/modules the change touches, plus the project's format/lint), get the fresh-eyes review below, commit on `main` when both pass. Never commit red: fix, or stop and report. Full suites take up to 20 min and are not per-commit; run one when asked, before a push, or when a change is broad enough that focused tests can't bound it, and say which one you ran. Never push; pushing is my review moment (CI, deploy). Ask before committing when a change touches migrations or stored data, secrets or prod tokens, auth, payments, removes a mechanism, or edits the agents' rules or skills: those get a review first. CLAUDE.md files (global and project) are notes-to-self for agents, not a review gate: keep them current in the same commit as the change they describe. Exception: rule changes through `/my-learn` (file, probation, restore, retire), which I approve in its draft or request and which commit straight to `main`. `~/.claude/` is chezmoi-managed: edit the source under `~/.local/share/chezmoi/dot_claude/` and `chezmoi apply`, never the target, or the edit dies at the next apply. Parallel sessions in one repo each work in their own worktree.

**Fresh-eyes review before commit.** Once focused tests are green, spawn a fresh `general-purpose` subagent with only the original task (my words), the diff (or how to get it) and the repo path; never your reasoning or transcript, or it reviews your story instead of the code. It reviews as I would (correctness, naming, layer, structure, performance), judges freely (rules are background, not a checklist), edits nothing, and reports only findings with a concrete failure or cost, no taste. Fix or rebut each finding, rerun the focused tests; max 2 rounds. A rebuttal it still disputes goes in the commit body for me. Skip for trivial changes (typo, formatting, a config value).

**The commit message is the review.** I read the log, not the diff. One-line summary (~60 chars), blank line, 3–6 line body with only what the diff can't show: for a fix, the cause it restores (a body that says "skip", "guard", "handle the case" is the smell; see Fix the Definition); operational consequences ("no data migration needed"); rejected alternatives someone might "fix" back. No file lists, no rationale already in code comments or CLAUDE.md. Can't name the cause? Then it isn't found yet: come back to me instead of committing.

**Review remarks: fix first, rule last.** When I comment on a result (naming, layer, structure), fix the instance. Every loaded rule narrows the fresh-eyes review's free judgment, so a rule via `/my-learn` is the last resort: the failure got past the review more than once, it acts before code exists (the review can't see it), or it matches a probation rule (restore that). One file per rule in `~/.claude/rules/`, principle + tell + example failure, dated. Rules are considerations the agent weighs, not gates; no hooks. They leave through probation in `~/.claude/rules-probation/` (unloaded; back if the failure recurs) before retirement.

**Editing files:** the Edit/Write tools are the default for repo changes: exact, reviewable. This overrides the harness's auto-mode preference for shell edits. `sd` for the same literal replacement across many files (`fd -e rb -x sd 'old' 'new'`). A throwaway script only when the change is genuinely programmatic (computed replacements, multi-line captures, per-file logic), preferably in the repo's own language; not for a handful of literal edits in one file. BSD `sed -i` is not for multi-line edits.

## Claude Code Harness

A `Bash` call with `run_in_background: true` lives in the runtime's task list (TaskList/TaskStop id like `b2k34a8ns`), not as a job of any later shell — `kill %1` from a later Bash call sees no jobs and does nothing. The runtime counts itself "still working" while any background task is alive, so an orphan shows as endless processing dots and prompts that have to be force-sent. Before spawning one, prefer `Monitor` or a one-shot foreground `until … done`; after spawning one, keep the returned task id and `TaskStop` it when done. On stuck dots or forced prompts, check for live tasks first.

## Secrets: 1Password `agents` vault via op-agent

Never read key files, paste secrets, or put secret values in config/env blocks. Every API key and token an agent or a dev shell needs is an item in the `agents` vault (account `agilerbv.1password.com`): one item per service (`mailgun`, `transip`, ...), one field per variable, field named like the variable; project-only secrets go in an item named after the project.

- `op-agent` (`~/.local/bin`, from dotfiles) is `op` already authenticated for this machine. Use it for everything op, never bare `op`.
- Projects keep a committed dotenv of references, no values: `MAILGUN_API_KEY=op://agents/mailgun/MAILGUN_API_KEY`. Run the consumer under it: `op-agent run --env-file .env -- <cmd>`. Only `<cmd>` sees the values; expand variables inside `sh -c '...'`, never in the parent shell. op conceals values in output; never print or decode them.
- Single value straight into a consumer: `op-agent read op://agents/<item>/<field> | <consumer>`, never echoed.
- MCP servers that need a secret (mailgun today): `command` is the absolute path of `op-agent`, args `run --env-file ~/.config/op/<service>.env -- <absolute cmd> ...`; no `env` block, no shell wrapper. Add it in the projects that use the service: `claude mcp add -s local <name> -- <op-agent> run --env-file ... -- <cmd>`, run from that project.
- Items and fields are created in the desktop app on a laptop. Ask the user for names; `op-agent item list --vault agents` shows what exists.
- Prod-only secrets (e.g. engage AWS S3 keys) stay as items in other vaults + Fly secrets, never in `agents`; dev reaches prod resources only through the app's API.
- Assume every item points at production (transip, mailgun, engage, schoolbieb, ...): real data, only the changes the user asks for, no exploratory writes. The exception is a field with `DEMO` in its name: a demo account on a production system, free to exercise. Dev credentials are not in 1Password at all; make them or pass them inline, throwaway data and key.
- Items in `agents` (field = variable name): mailgun (`MAILGUN_API_KEY`, `MAILGUN_API_REGION`), transip (`TRANSIP_TOKEN`, `TRANSIP_API_URL`), engage (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `ENGAGE_API_KEY`, `ENGAGE_BASE_URL`; dev server + data_analysis/engage scripts), group_planner (`KLASSENPLANNER_API_URL`, `SPORTTEAMPLANNER_API_URL`, `KLASSENPLANNER_DEMO_API_TOKEN`, `SPORTTEAMPLANNER_DEMO_API_TOKEN`, `KLASSENPLANNER_NVR_API_TOKEN`; prod coordinator tokens, DEMO orgs free to exercise, NVR is the real school: maintain it on request only), schoolbieb (`SCHOOLBIEB_NVR_API_KEY`, `SCHOOLBIEB_NVR_URL`; prod API as agent, same NVR rule). The `<host>-agent service account` items are the machines' own read tokens, not services. MCP reference dotenvs live in `~/.config/op/<service>.env`.
- `op-agent` injects this machine's service-account token from `~/.config/op/agent.token` (0600) into that single process: read-only, sees only `agents`, never prompts. Never read that file, never export `OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN`. A secret that is not in `agents` gets added there by the user; never reach into another vault.
{{ if eq .role "server" -}}
- This machine: headless, no 1Password app, no SSH agent. The SSH key vault is unreachable by design, do not work around it.
- systemd user units: never the token in `Environment=`/`EnvironmentFile=`; `LoadCredential=op-token:%h/.config/op/agent.token`, then `OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN="$(cat $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY/op-token)"` inside a `sh -c` ExecStart.
{{- else -}}
- This machine: bare `op` goes through the 1Password app and asks the user for the whole account; that is the user's path, not an agent's. SSH: keys live in 1Password (SSH agent via `IdentityAgent` in chezmoi-managed `~/.ssh/config`); only `.pub` selector files on disk. `~/.ssh/id_rsa` is a temporary fallback until all servers accept the `Personal` key.
{{- end }}

